{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And her young ones lap up blood</b> — like: ושתו ולעו (and they drank and lapped). The meaning: they swallow the blood of the slain bodies that [the eagle] brings. And the first ayin is doubled, or it is from the derivation על״א in rabbinic language. And the meaning: they break the ribs of the slain to extract blood. The first [explanation] is correct in grammar, and the latter too, for it is from a noun root, and this is a well-known matter: for the eagle sees from a very distant place the location of the slain and runs there."], :book :job, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {0 "Resolved the unspecified root reference על״א as rabbinic language per Ibn Ezra's explicit phrase לשון רבנן; the referent is the Aramaic/root noun behind the form."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Job -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/eyov/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:05:21.710707487Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}